notes={
'A01':dict(purpose='Distinguish single-cell silence from a pooled rate estimate with the same mean.',prereqs='Foundations SPIKES defines rate and neuron index N. POISSON gives K, lambda=r Delta t, mean/variance and the20Hz/50ms example before this image.',order='The original population calculation motivates the comparison, the intro names normalization, the caption explains probability stems, and the interpretation returns to per-cell rate before recurrent conditional independence is qualified.',question='Why do the two estimates share a20Hz mean but have very different possible values and spread?',answer='K/(N Delta t) preserves mean r. One count changes the estimate by20Hz for N1 and1Hz for N20; independent variances average down, yielding SD20 versus4.47Hz. Shared state fluctuations are a separate issue handled next.'),
'A02':dict(purpose='Separate one random path, its distribution-wide RMS scale and fixed-bias displacement.',prereqs='The same section defines independent increments, Brownian motion, MSD2Dt and RMS sqrt(2Dt). It then computes the chosen D,v and4s crossover before the image.',order='The prior paragraph supplies squared contributions; the figure explicitly switches to their square-root displacement scales. The final text now uses the defined RMS phrase, then the next paragraph develops covariance across times.',question='Why can a path approach its starting value while the plotted random scale rises, and when does bias dominate?',answer='A realized path can reverse. RMS averages squared displacements over possible paths and grows as sqrt(t); constant velocity gives vt, so the chosen positive scales cross at4s. Neither an RMS envelope nor these chosen values is an animal-data boundary.'),
'A03':dict(purpose='Show why added constant baseline firing can reduce local Poisson information despite more spikes.',prereqs='Foundations introduces tuning and baseline. MEMORY-FI derives squared derivative divided by rate, explains positive-rate regularity and instantaneous internal-state meaning, then introduces these controlled curves.',order='The preceding claim about peak rates is tested by two analytic positive curves, their shared derivative and their contributions. Interpretation explains the visible difference, then the text begins inverse-information estimator error.',question='Why do both information curves vanish at the peak, and why is the higher-rate curve less informative here?',answer='At the peak the local derivative is zero. Elsewhere squared derivative is the same for both curves, but the higher baseline increases the Poisson variance/rate denominator. This is about these chosen curves, not a proved intervention in a recurrent network.'),
'A04':dict(purpose='Show the difference between an actual diffusion value and its information-derived lower bound.',prereqs='The chapter has derived D>=1/(2tau squared J), its model assumptions and state-dependent equality condition. The immediately preceding two-cell calculation gives the coordinate units and the0/100 examples.',order='The image completes the scalar comparison before the text extends information to a matrix. Its caption keeps the activation coordinate distinct from angle and excludes zero-rate boundaries.',question='Does larger information at coordinate100 make this circuit diffuse more slowly?',answer='No. Actual D remains1000 while the permitted minimum becomes750. The gap is inequality slack in this model; high information allows low D but does not guarantee it.'),
'A05':dict(purpose='Explain small single-cell correlation alongside a large common movement-variance fraction.',prereqs='EYE-CHI defines X=C+epsilon,Y=C+eta, calibrated unit gain, pairwise uncorrelated contributions, R and chi, and computes the exact constructed variance values before insertion.',order='The prior paragraph distinguishes R squared from common fraction; bars and a varying-private-noise curve make the denominator difference visible. The following paragraph returns to uncertainty in empirical chi estimates.',question='How can R be.17 when the common contribution is82percent of movement variance?',answer='The individual predictor contains much more private variance, enlarging sqrt[Var(X)Var(Y)] while covariance remains Var(C). The movement denominator is much smaller. This reasoning depends on the stated gain and covariance assumptions and does not locate noise uniquely in an integrator.'),
'A06':dict(purpose='Show what measurement-offset subtraction changes and why the corrected small value remains uncertain.',prereqs='EYE-MEASUREMENT-NOISE defines sampled position errors, derives the positive-lag2sigma_m squared offset and exact-zero exception, and gives the numeric subtraction and correlated-error alternative.',order='A declared Brownian illustration displays that offset; revised caption and alt keep the open endpoint separate from actual zero. Interpretation says this corrects an average, and the next section asks about corrected time dependence.',question='Why is zero lag different, and why is.0001 uncertainty substantial after subtraction?',answer='At zero, the same error sample cancels itself; distinct independent errors contribute twice the position-error variance. Subtracting.0010 from.0014 leaves.0004, so.0001 is25percent of the remainder. Individual trajectory errors remain unknown.'),
'A07':dict(purpose='Make the fixed Gaussian input and spatial threshold-to-rate operation concrete before counting fields.',prereqs='PLACE-GP defines Gaussian processes, normalization z, q,a,the positive-part operation, excursion fields and sigma. The chosen anchor follows the sigma/covariance paragraph.',order='The chosen input/gain/threshold are introduced before the aligned panels; interpretation preserves fixed space versus moving time. The next section now counts the same connected intervals that have just been pictured.',question='What changes when the input is thresholded, and what stays fixed across repeated visits?',answer='At every position, rate is six times the positive excess over.8. Subthreshold positions fire at zero and the remaining support separates into fields. The same input/rate map remains fixed over space; visit-to-visit spikes are not drawn.'),
'A08':dict(purpose='Separate unit-free size rescaling from the power transformation that makes the limiting law exponential.',prereqs='The earlier rare-peak section defines exponential U; PLACE-SIZE-LAW derives p_d(s), beta and its units before this image. The added intro defines w. Caption now explains the first survival/density terms directly.',order='Units are established, then scale is removed with w; the plot shows remaining shape differences and U coincidence. Interpretation says the exponential was an assumption of the calculation, followed by the existing size-law table and no-point-mass explanation.',question='Why do the densities differ after removing the size scale but their transformed survival probabilities agree?',answer='w removes beta but retains the d-dependent Jacobian. Raising w to2/d gives U and cancels that shape difference, producing exp(-u). The3D density diverges near zero yet integrates to one; this is the high-threshold limiting identity, not evidence of zero-volume fields or a fresh experimental collapse.'),
'A09':dict(purpose='Connect fixed tuning to common rate changes without inventing inhibitory wiring or a joint tested circuit.',prereqs='SYNTHESIS-JOINT-CALCULATION defines R_i=f_i(x(t)), local Taylor change and covariance2Dt f_i prime f_j prime. The two-cell values and single-source qualification precede the added baseline choice.',order='The local construction is labeled before the example, and the extra10Hz baseline is declared before the upper absolute-rate panel. The lower plane shows changes; interpretation leads to the original need for joint measurements.',question='Why does one common position error produce perfectly opposite changes, and what would extra private noise do?',answer='One scalar displacement multiplies slopes2 and-1, forcing delta R2=-delta R1/2 and shared-source correlation-1. Independent private noise adds variation away from that line. Opposite slopes imply negative covariance here without proving an inhibitory connection.')}

